,{"componentCode":"transaction_table","description":"Typography used for table header and cell text.","name":"table typography","type":"typography"},{"componentCode":"transaction_table","description":"Horizontal/vertical padding and row spacing for the table.","name":"table spacing","type":"spacing"}]},"resolvingGaps":{"decisions":[{"affectedComponentCodes":["transaction_table"],"code":"sortable_headers_as_buttons_with_aria_sort","decision":"Implement each sortable column header as a <button type="button"> inside the <th>. The button accessible name is "Sort by {ColumnName}". Maintain sort state in the table as {sortKey, sortDirection}. Apply aria-sort to the <th> of the active sort column only: "ascending" or "descending"; all other headers have no aria-sort attribute. The header button shows a visual sort indicator (up/down chevron) and includes visually-hidden text that announces "sorted ascending/descending" for the active column.","rationale":"This makes sorting explicitly keyboard-operable and screen-reader discoverable using standard table semantics with aria-sort, while keeping the rest of the headers neutral. Using a button in the header is the most conservative, widely supported pattern for interactive sorting.","sourceGap":"Sorting controls are not specified as keyboard-operable header buttons with proper aria-sort and accessible names"},{"affectedComponentCodes":["transaction_table"],"code":"sort_state_defaults_and_toggle_behavior","decision":"Default sort is Date descending on initial render. Sorting is single-column only (no multi-sort). Clicking/activating a header toggles direction between ascending and descending for that column; selecting a different column sets that column to ascending on first activation. There is no unsorted state once data is shown.","rationale":"Defines deterministic sorting behavior needed for implementation and testing. Choosing a common default (most recent first) and a simple two-state toggle avoids ambiguous "none" state handling and multi-sort complexity.","sourceGap":"Sorting state details: default sort column/direction, unsorted state, and sort direction indicator behavior"},{"affectedComponentCodes":["transaction_table"],"code":"row_keyboard_access_via_dedicated_details_link","decision":"Do not make the entire <tr> focusable. Add a dedicated "View details" action as the last focusable element within each row (e.g., a <a> or <button>) with accessible name "View details for transaction {merchant} on {date}" (fallback to "View transaction details" if values missing). Mouse click anywhere on the row also triggers the same action, but keyboard access is provided via the dedicated control.","rationale":"Provides a clear, standard keyboard path without relying on nonstandard focusable row semantics, while preserving the brief’s row-click convenience for pointer users. This conservatively reduces accessibility risk and complexity.","sourceGap":"Row-click interaction lacks keyboard equivalent (Enter/Space) and focusable row/interactive element semantics"},{"affectedComponentCodes":["transaction_table","transaction_pagination"],"code":"focus_visible_styles_required","decision":"Apply a visible focus indicator to sortable header buttons, the per-row "View details" control, and all pagination controls using :focus-visible. The focus ring must be at least 2px and not removed; it must be visible against both header and body backgrounds.","rationale":"Explicitly defines focus-visible styling targets so downstream implementation and QA can verify keyboard navigation. Keeping the requirement generic (thickness/visibility) is conservative given missing design tokens.","sourceGap":"Focus visible styling is not specified for sortable headers, rows, and pagination controls"},{"affectedComponentCodes":["transaction_status_badge"],"code":"status_badge_non_color_and_forced_colors_behavior","decision":"Status badge must always render the status text label (Pending/Completed/Failed/Refunded) and never re