sers"},{"affectedComponentCodes":["transaction_table_pagination"],"code":"pagination_controls_labels_and_focus","decision":"Pagination includes: Previous button, Next button, and a page size <select> with options 25/50/100. Wrap controls in a <nav aria-label="Transaction table pagination">. Previous/Next have aria-label "Previous page" / "Next page" and use disabled=true at boundaries. Render current page text "Page X of Y" as an aria-live="polite" region that updates on page changes. Tab order is Previous -> Next -> Page size selector.","rationale":"This specifies explicit labels, boundary disabled behavior, and a deterministic focus order while keeping the UI minimal and consistent with the brief (no requirement for page numbers/first/last).","sourceGap":"Pagination controls need explicit labeling for screen readers (current page, next/previous, page size selector) and focus order behavior"},{"affectedComponentCodes":["transaction_status_badge"],"code":"status_badge_text_not_color_only","decision":"Always render the status text inside the badge (e.g., "Pending"). The badge element includes aria-label identical to the visible text. Do not rely on color to convey meaning; color is supplementary only.","rationale":"Keeping status text present ensures non-visual users receive the same information and resolves the risk of color-only encoding without requiring extra UI elements.","sourceGap":"Status conveyed by color only is possible; specify text/aria-label remains present and ensure badge meaning is announced non-visually"},{"affectedComponentCodes":["transaction_table","transaction_table_pagination"],"code":"focus_visible_styles","decision":"Apply a consistent focus-visible ring to interactive elements: sortable header buttons, row overlay links, pagination buttons, and the page size select. Use a 2px outline with 2px offset and ensure it is visible on all status badge background colors.","rationale":"Explicit focus-visible styling ensures keyboard usability across all interactive controls without prescribing a full theme, and it is a conservative accessibility baseline.","sourceGap":"Specify focus-visible styling for interactive elements (headers, rows, pagination) to meet keyboard usability"},{"affectedComponentCodes":["transaction_table"],"code":"loading_empty_error_states","decision":"Add three table-level states: (1) Loading: show 10-row skeleton placeholders while retaining the table header; disable sorting and pagination controls. (2) Empty: show a single full-width row message "No transactions"; keep headers visible and hide pagination. (3) Error: show a full-width message "Transactions couldn’t be loaded" with a "Retry" button that re-triggers fetch; keep headers visible and disable sorting/pagination until success.","rationale":"These states cover the primary data lifecycle gaps (loading/empty/error) in a way that preserves layout stability and defines control availability for downstream implementation and tests.","sourceGap":"Loading state for initial fetch and for sort/pagination transitions (skeleton/placeholder vs spinner)"},{"affectedComponentCodes":["transaction_table"],"code":"sort_behavior_defaults_and_nulls","decision":"Default sort is Date descending. Sorting is bi-state per column: selecting a column toggles ascending/descending; selecting a different column activates that column in ascending order. Sorting is stable. Null/blank values always sort last in both directions.","rationale":"This fully specifies predictable sort rules (default, toggling, stability, null handling) with conservative choices commonly used in transaction tables.","sourceGap":"Define sorting rules per column: default sort column/direction, tri-state vs bi-state toggling, stable sort behavior, and how null/blank values sort"},{"affectedComponentCodes":["transaction_table_pagination"],"code":"pagination_page_size_change_resets","decision":"When page size changes (25/50/100), reset to page 1 and refetch/recompute the dataset for that new